{{also|anti-matter}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|anti|matter}}. {{coin|en|Arthur Schuster|nat=British|occ=physicist|nobycat=1|in=1898}} to describe matter that resists gravity in a jocular article in ''{{w|Nature (journal)|Nature}}'' titled "Potential Matter.—A Holiday Dream", but not used in a modern sense until the 1940s.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|anti-matter}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæntimæˌtɚ/|/ˈæn.taɪ.mæˌtɚ/}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|physics}} [[matter|Matter]] that is composed of the [[antiparticle]]s of those that [[constitute]] normal matter.
# {{lb|en|physics}} A form of matter that has a key property, such as [[charge]], opposite to that of ordinary matter.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|antimatter rocket}}

